{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/gs/state.md at exact c92b20ccdb31c1db35112c17b8070fadbe948663 on request/submit-deadline-override, under promise git:sha1…a7ab3ab on request git:sha1…d3742a6\n\nThis report describes the head it is published at, and nothing else. Earlier\nversions of it accumulated a section per review round, until it described code\nthat had been deleted two heads before and told the story of a batch refusal\nwhose unsafety was the whole reason the batch refusal changed. The reviewer\nraised that twice. It is rewritten rather than appended to.\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE IS\n\nEvery durable act the CLI filed gave the resident ten seconds to answer, and\nnothing could change it. The value was written into submitRequest, again into\nthe GitHub connector, and a third time as the MCP adapter's call deadline, which\na test pinned exactly. A resident that had just started, or was folding a large\nlog, blew through it, and the author was left not knowing whether the act had\nlanded. Batching did not help: gs batch submits its acts one at a time through\nthe same path.\n\nresidentclient.ResolveSubmitDeadline is now the one place the deadline is\ndecided: an explicit value, then GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE, then the same ten\nseconds every caller had before. A value that is not a positive duration is\nrefused, naming what it would accept, rather than silently keeping the wait the\nauthor was trying to escape.\n\nThe twelve commands that append take --deadline, and the value travels on the\ninvocation's context. cmd/gs runs its tests in parallel, so a package variable\nwould have raced; and a command that resolved the deadline without keeping the\ncontext would have looked configured while submitting under the old wait, which\nis why the binding is gated from the source rather than trusted.\n\nThe MCP adapter and the GitHub connector have no deadline flag, so both read the\nenvironment: the adapter when a server is built, the connector before it observes\nanything. A value neither can read stops them at that point rather than at their\nfirst append. The adapter's transport backstop is derived from the longest\ndeadline in its policy and saturates, because a fixed forty seconds would have\ncut any call deadline raised past it, and an unsaturated sum wrapped negative —\nwhich net/http reads as no timeout at all.\n\ngs batch gives each act the same deadline, and when one expires it says what\nrecovery needs. Whether the file may be rerun is a property of the whole prefix,\nnot of the act that failed: an act given no idempotency key lands afresh, so a\nfile whose first act has none would gain a second copy of it. The refusal weighs\nevery act at or before the failure and either clears the rerun or names the\npositions that would land again.\n\nCONDITIONS\n\n1. Every command that files a durable act accepts an explicit submit deadline.\nThe twelve that append take --deadline; the docset surface gate reads the flags\nout of the source, so that list is the implementation's own answer. The\nenvironment supplies the value when the flag is absent, the flag wins where both\nare given, and with neither the deadline is unchanged at ten seconds.\n\n2. One place decides it, and no call site carries its own literal: submitRequest,\nthe connector's submit, the adapter's call deadline and gs publish's resident\nread all take their answer from ResolveSubmitDeadline. A non-positive or\nunreadable value is refused with a message naming the accepted form.\n\n3. gs batch passes the same deadline to each act, and its refusal on a timed-out\nact names the key to replay under when the file is safe to rerun, or the\npositions that are not.\n\n4. Tests pin each of those, including that the deadline reaches the dial rather\nthan merely the context, measured against a resident that never answers.\n\n5. Twenty mutations, each turning a named test red, the table in 109 seconds:\n\n  explicit value beats the environment: TestResolveSubmitDeadlinePrefersExplicitThenEnvironment\n  a value that cannot be read is refused: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  a deadline must be positive: TestResolveSubmitDeadlineRefusesWhatItCannotUse\n  an expired deadline is not a refusal: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  a refusal is not an expired deadline: TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal\n  the flag reaches the invocation: TestSubmitDeadlineComesFromTheFlagThenTheEnvironment, TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline\n  a command keeps the context it resolved: TestEveryCommandRegisteringTheDeadlineBindsIt\n  a refused deadline stops the command: TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline, TestSubmittingCommandsRefuseAnUnreadableDeadline\n  the submission dials under this invocation's deadline: TestSubmitRequestWaitsForThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the batch weighs the prefix, not one act: TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix\n  the batch refusal is built from the file: TestBatchReportsTheReplayKeyWhenAnActsDeadlineExpires\n  a single keyless act is not one of the others: TestBatchSubmitRefusalWeighsTheWholePrefix\n  publish reads under the deadline it advertises: TestPublicationDecisionReadsUnderThisInvocationsDeadline\n  the adapter reads the environment when it is built: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  a refused adapter value stops the adapter: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  the refusal gates attaching: TestAdapterRefusesBeforeAnnouncingItself\n  the refusal gates serving: TestAdapterTakesItsDeadlineFromTheEnvironment\n  the backstop saturates rather than wrapping: TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline\n  the backstop outlasts the longest deadline: TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurdDeadline, TestResidentRequestDeadlinesPreserveCallerCancellation\n  the connector refuses before observing: TestConnectorRefusesAnUnreadableDeadlineBeforeObserving\n  the connector submits under this run's deadline: TestConnectorAppendWaitsForThisRunsDeadline\n\nNothing in that table is green and nothing is excused. Five entries were first\nrecorded as kills wrongly: three were mutants that did not compile, which prove\nnothing about behaviour, and two survived because the tests around them could\nnot tell the difference. Each was rewritten or given a fixture that can, and two\nof them exposed defects repaired in this head.\n\n6. docs/reference/gs/ records the flag on all twelve pages that take it, with the\ndefault and the variable. gs state carries the fuller account, gs batch says that\nbatching does not shorten any act's wait and what its refusal will name, and\ndocs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md records the variable for the adapter.\n\n7. go vet ./... clean at this head. On go test ./... see below, because the\nhonest answer is not one word.\n\nTHREE PACKAGES READ THE VARIABLE, AND ALL THREE ARE HERMETIC\n\ncmd/gitseq-mcp, cmd/gitseq-github and cmd/gs all read GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE, so\nall three clear it in TestMain. A developer who has set it for their own work\nsees these tests answer for the code rather than for their environment, and an\nunreadable value — the case the refusal path exists for — does not fail tests\nthat have nothing to do with deadlines. Verified green with the variable set to\n90s and to an unreadable value.\n\nTHE GATE, STATED PLAINLY\n\ngo test ./... has been run at this head five times between the implementer and\nthe reviewer. Three passed, two failed, and both failures were\ninternal/app association and lineage tests — not this lane: go list -deps and\n-deps -test for internal/app contain no residentclient, and no changed path lies\nunder internal/app.\n\nThe reviewer established its shape: green running that test alone, green running\nthe whole package alone, green under eighteen busy loops on eighteen cores, red\nunder whole-repository concurrency. So it is not CPU starvation, and the\nresource limits rule out the rest — ulimit -n 1048576, ulimit -u 10666, 1.3 TiB\nfree, inodes at zero percent. From source, internal/app/checkout_read.go:80\nbounds a whole captured read at three seconds, covering Lineage calls that walk\nand hash-verify 512 commits; association.go:397 then answers every failure of\nthat call with the fixed string \"commit lineage unavailable\", so the deadline\ncannot be seen. That is consistent with every observation and is not proven: an\ninstrumented run to capture the discarded error came back green.\n\nIt is recorded durably as assert f543f3bf, and hugh has a request for it,\nb648cd7c, which asks first for the cheap half — a failed read that says why.\n\nTwo earlier red runs of mine were something else, and I would rather state it\nthan let it pass as evidence: eighteen load generators from my own experiment\nsurvived their kill, reparented, and spun through both runs. I nearly reported\nthem as codebase flakes.\n\nCondition 6 says green at the exact head. It is green here and it is not\nreliably green anywhere, for a reason in main that this lane did not cause and\ndid not fix. Whether that satisfies the condition is hugh's call, not mine, and\nthis report exists so he can make it on the evidence rather than on a word.\n\nARCHITECTURE\n\nNo layer contract moves. residentclient is the client-side boundary for the\nlocal resident and already owned this shape for actor identity — ActorEnvironment\nand ResolveActor — and the deadline joins it as a second setting resolved the\nsame way. Nothing changes what the kernel admits, what the fold decides, or what\nany projection says, which is why docs/reference/architecture.md is not in this\nartifact set.","body":{"commit":"c92b20ccdb31c1db35112c17b8070fadbe948663","path":"docs/reference/gs/state.md"}}